A||N||10||Toogy||Lowering a Fiero||||4||
Z||000000||Toogy||12-13-2001||09:06 AM||||Hey All,<P>I am still 3 months aways from getting my 87 GT.  I am seriously thinking about lowering it once I get it.  I don't have a bunch of money to use coilovers and drop spindles.  I guess I just want to know if people are reasonalby happy with using just drop springs?  Will my ride be hell?  I can't imagine it would be that bad.<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR><B>Soon to be: 1987 GT 5-speed</B>||||reg||4
Z||000001||Nashco||12-13-2001||10:12 AM||||Springs will be fine, as long as you're not expecting perfect geometry (depending on the spring size you may not be able to get it aligned properly) and you don't care too much about the ride.  Lowering springs are going to be somewhat stiffer than the factory springs.  Personally, I don't mind it at all.  If you're tight on cash and you want it to be low....springs are pretty much your option.<P>Honestly, you should consider just cutting the stock springs.  Anywhere from half a coil to a full coil cut off will lower it and retain a good ride.  Some people say cutting your springs is stupid, but some suspension experts actually recommend it for a non-competition car.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Toogy||12-13-2001||10:20 AM||||Thanks,<P>So if I go with the lowering springs, what else do I need to do?  I mean will my camber be really screwed?  Will my tires suffer in the long run?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Nashco||12-13-2001||10:44 AM||||Depends on how far you want to lower it.  1" springs can be compensated for with the factory alignments stuff (assuming all else is in good shape).  Most 1.5" springs you'll be alright, but when you get to 2" you're probably going to have to make a sacrifice somewhere with the alignment.<P>If you want to get a big drop (2") spring, you have to get some offset bushings to compensate.  You can get these from 8shark.com; they're UHMW, so they'll give you a very tight suspension, but a rougher ride.<P>In the long run, depends on if your alignment is within specs.  Your camber will hardly be effected with 1" springs, but the more drop you get from springs the more camber adjustment you'll need.<P>What're you planning?  Are you looking for a cheap thing to do with no side effects, or are you actually willing to spend some money?  What you can expect all depends on what you want to do.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||TRiAD||12-13-2001||11:23 AM||||Anytime you lower your car you will need to have it realigned. If you go with Eibachs, (or similar non-insane drop) you should be able to get it back close to spec. <BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0NgAAAMsSN*Wryq60N*CeWB!hXjfeIaMEc1YxPH9aPzaIXm!17ZpdBFE0Yk*d*dHNFG*GR3SFyLa8KJgh1IKe2Q/xmas-sig.jpg">       <BR><B><I><FONT COLOR="red">Merry Christmas!</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">Project MR1 ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</B><BR>Photoshop rendition of planned modifications<BR>AIM ~ Michael Blue 72</I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||ejayerik||12-13-2001||06:28 PM||||boing-boing-boing-bounce-bounce-bounce....SLAM!||||reg||7
Z||000006||2birds||12-13-2001||08:12 PM||||I like my 86 GT much better since it has been lowered.  I used Suspension Techniques linear rate springs.  The Eibach's are variable rate, but they cost more.  I'd go with the Eibach's if I had the chance to do it again.  I also rebuilt the entire suspension with poly and new balljoints at the same time I installed the springs.  The handling difference is like night and day ver the original, and I've had mine since it was 2 years old.<P>Use the Moog Problem Solver upper balljoints (slotted bolt holes) so you can get the front camber perfect.  Mine cost $20 a piece 6 years ago from a local parts store.  The rear is easy if you have aftermarket struts, or the camber kits others have used.<P>The biggest problem is going to be finding a shop with an alignment rack you can get on without bottoming out, and that will take the time to do the job right.  Some places won't touch a lowered Fiero without charging by the hour.  The Moog balljoints are a PITA for the tech's to work with, but that's too bad for them, IMHO. <P>I'll e-mail you a picture if you want to see how low it is now.  Let me know if you want it.||||reg||1
Z||000007||artherd||12-13-2001||08:29 PM||||Don't cut the (front) springs if you have an 84-87, it will be horrible.<P>88 cars are a completely different story, cut springs (max of 1 coil or so, mine are cut exactly one full coil.) work very well indeed.  Infact, on 88s, skip the drop-springs all together.   <P>Or go full coilover, you won't regret it!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000008||artherd||12-13-2001||08:29 PM||||Don't cut the (front) springs if you have an 84-87, it will be horrible.<P>88 cars are a completely different story, cut springs (max of 1 coil or so, mine are cut exactly one full coil.) work very well indeed.  Infact, on 88s, skip the drop-springs all together.   <P>Or go full coilover, you won't regret it!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||shark93726||12-13-2001||09:26 PM||||If you cut the springs, cut off some of the rubber bump stop that is inside each coil spring.  Try and cut as much off as possible, but leave a "V" shaped piece sticking down.  That will still help keep the suspension from bottoming hard against the frame, which can actually bend things, but not stiffen up the ride so much from little bumps.<P>If you don't reduce the rubber bumpers, the suspension ends up practically "riding" on them all the time, which gives a really "rough" ride.<P>------------------<BR>Gerald Storvik (8shark.com)||||reg||1
Z||000010||fieroX||12-15-2001||03:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Don't cut the (front) springs if you have an 84-87, it will be horrible.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I cut 1.5 coils off my 87, front and rear.  It looks and rides 90% normal.  It is a little bouncy, but It handles 200% better, and looks 1000% better.  It might not handle as well as some competition suspensions do, but Ill just put my money into my engine, and catch you in the straights!<P><P>------------------<BR>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fierox.com/logo2small.gif">  </A><BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroX.com</A><BR>coming soon L67 powered 87 coupe||||reg||1
